MID_SE_Dan_Set_C: <EDH>
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_001: *snore*
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_002: Dante? Dante! I'm talking to you.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_003: Mnyum... Ah, it's you. Why have you roused me\nso early?
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_004: Early? It's evening. Have you been asleep all day?
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_005: The characters from my play spent all of last night\nraising a ruckus in my head.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_006: It was fine grist for the drama mill, but also meant\nI got very little sleep.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_007: That makes no sense to me.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_008: Can't you get it together, if only for today?
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_009: Everyone's a critic. I need no mothering from the\nlikes of you.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_010: Your behavior is so outrageous that I beg to differ.\nAnd can't you at least say "fathering"?
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_011: You? My father? I shall not entertain the notion even\nin metaphorical terms.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_012: My father is perhaps the only person I truly respect.\nEveryone else pales in comparison.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_013: It's good that you respect someone, at least. Honoring\nyour father shows a glimmer of decency.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_014: Thank you for the compliment, however backhanded.\nBut I am not his only admirer.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_015: My father, Aeneus, is one of Dagda's most celebrated\npoets. Surely you've heard of him?
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_016: Hm? No, I can't say I have.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_017: What?! You haven't read the tragic masterpiece of\nthe loss of one's love leading to the loss of one's life?
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_018: The legendary poem that inspired youth the world\nover to cling a little tighter to their loves?!
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_019: Sorry, but I'm not familiar with it. Perhaps because\nI'm not from here.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_020: You've never chanced across it? Impossible!
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_021: Aeneus's poems are extensively quoted in the plays\nby yours truly!
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_022: How could even an outsider be ignorant of both my\nbeloved plays and my father's cherished poems?!
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_023: It's clearly possible. I'm the proof.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_024: Ughhhh! You must be one of those artless drudges\nwith no interest in dramaturgy.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_025: I can't stand your ilk. May the goddess Kalla curse\nyou with scratchy nibs!
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_026: That's rather rude. I'll have you know I dabble in\nfable-writing now and again.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_027: They may not be up to your standards, but that\ndoesn't mean I'm uncultured.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_028: Truly? Then I propose we show each other our work\nand exchange feedback.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_C_029: Agreed. Let us see what you've got.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B: <EDH>
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_001: Virgili... I hope you find happiness.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_002: Salutations, Seteth. I see you've been enjoying\nmy magnum opus.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_003: Yes. The care your protagonist showed for his\ndaughter brought tears to my eyes.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_004: I knew The Rise of the White-Maned Demon was\nacclaimed, but I didn't know you had this in you.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_005: Did you think so lightly of me? Comedy, tragedy,\nadventure—no matter the genre, I'm never generic.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_006: But I'm less experienced with fables, so the one you\nshared was most enlightening.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_007: Points off for the preachy tone, but a passing\ngrade overall.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_008: Was that supposed to be a compliment?
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_009: Obviously! That counts as a glowing review from\na master auteur like me!
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_010: Creative works have tremendous influential power,\nas your story clearly shows.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_011: It's proof that great art can come from thoroughly\nunlikable artists.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_012: Ha. I'm glad you gave it fair consideration.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_013: I'll go ahead and overlook this masterpiece play's\nastonishingly messy author in return.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_014: Ahahaha! The audacity! I find this tête-à-tête\nof ours highly amusing.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_015: But I cannot forgive your ignorance of Aeneus.\nI'll gift you one of his folios to edify yourself.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_016: Oh, I've read it now. It wasn't hard to find a copy—\nlots of merchants stock his work.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_017: The poems were profoundly touching. I'm almost\njealous that you got to grow up with them.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_018: Ah, so you do have an eye for verse! And the\nhumility to speak of it.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_019: Should the chance arise, I might introduce you\nto my father.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_020: I would be honored.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_021: Though you should lower your expectations for\nbehavior beforehand.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_022: My father is even messier than I. He sleeps by day,\nwrites by night, and often goes out in his loincloth.
MID_SE_Dan_Set_B_023: Mmhm. That explains a lot, but...I'll try to hold\nmy tongue.